[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This little camera drops you straight into a 1980s video game world. This is Pixas, a pixel art camera that renders the world around you in a retro 8bit video game style. And if your first reaction is, why would I need this? Then'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is probably not the camera for you. But if this camera looks interesting to you, keep watching. So let\'s start with the camera itself. The Pixels is tiny.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It fits right in the palm of your hand. You can just carry it with you all the time. You can easily put it into your pocket or your bag. The body of the camera is 3D printed, which is also part'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of that quirky nerdy character. There are only two buttons on the camera. One is the power button and the other is the shutter button. It has a built-in rechargeable battery charge through'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'USBC. It takes a micro SD card and surprisingly there\'s even a tripod mount at the bottom, but this is one of those cameras I don\'t really think need a tripod mount. There\'s a tiny OD display'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the back. And when I say tiny, I mean tiny. It\'s only 1 in. Very low resolution as well. 128* 64 pixels. So only about 8,000 dots in total. and it\'s'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just black and white, not even grayscale. The screen is not that bright. So, when using it outdoors, you may not be able to see what\'s on the screen at all. Usually, when you\'re'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'taking photo using a normal camera, you will know exactly what photo you are going to capture by looking at the camera\'s LCD screen or UVF. But for these pictures, every time you press the'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shutter, you don\'t really know what exactly the photo is going to look like. The tiny black and white screen only give you a very rough idea of what you\'re going to capture. That is if you'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can see the screen. So most of the time I can\'t be bothered to get perfect framing or exposure or anything. I just point the camera and click. And strangely I think that\'s part of the'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'charm. I take a photo and wait until I got home and wait for the camera to surprise me. Sometimes it\'s a good surprise, sometimes it\'s a bad surprise, but it\'s always fun. Startup does take a'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'few seconds, so it\'s not exactly instant, but I think it\'s perfectly fine for what this camera is. Okay, now let\'s talk about image quality. Or maybe I shouldn\'t even call it image quality'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because what you captured are not really photographs in the traditional sense. These are pixel art, only 0.03 03 megapixels. The lens is 35 mm equivalent focal length and f1.2 aperture. But'

[02:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t expect to shoot anything with shallow depo field because of the tiny sensor which give you huge depo field. The camera has fixed focus like most toy cameras. The final image looks like some'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kind of 8bit computer game pixel art rather than a real photograph. And that\'s really the whole point of this camera. You\'re not getting realistic colors. You\'re not getting much detail'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at all. You are getting a tiny little piece of pixel art that roughly represents the scene you photograph. And I absolutely like it. One thing that surprised me is the aspect ratio.'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Instead of the old school 4 to3 aspect ratio that we often associate with old CRT screens and virtual video games, the Pixels actually outputs 2:1 aspect ratio, 256x'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '128 pixels output. While the 2:1 aspect ratio works well on a phone or a computer screen, I do wish there was a 4 to3 output option because if you\'re going to go all in on this virtual 8bit'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aesthetic, I think a 4 to3 mode would look fantastic. Give you that old CRT screen vibe. Now, not only the photos you capture have that retro 8bit'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'computer game vibe, the camera\'s manual system feels retro, too. I am really showing my age here, but it really reminds me of those old DOS command line system and software interfaces from the'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '1980s because apart from the power button, there\'s only one button to control the camera. So, it does take a bit of time to get used to the control system. A long press of the shutter'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'button brings up the setup menu. A short press move through the different options and a long press adds more like enter or select. It does make me wonder why they are not one or two more buttons to make'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the control easier. But then I feel they intentionally did that to give you that retro style control. That quirkiness just adds a bit of character and fun. Surprisingly, there are quite a few'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things you can adjust from the camera manual. For example, there is even a raw output setting. But raw basically means the original JPEG captured by the camera before all of those pixel art treatment'

[05:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is applied. You can create your own color palette and load it onto the camera. So, you can have black and white, muted colors, funky colors, or pretty much whatever you wanted. And'

[05:22] [scene:talk]
